domain StructuredSemanticAuthoringKernelDesignV1:
  description "Reasoning for accepted implementation-design revision6 as refined by accepted ADR0032 and ADR0033."
problem P1:
  |
    Fix implementation contracts that make focused structured semantic authoring
    typed, bounded, recoverable and reusable without activating a runtime route.
evidence E1:
  |
    Accepted ADR0032 incorporates unaffected ADR0031 decisions. Those decisions assign
    orchestration to a thin kernel, domain meaning to adapters, effects to ports, and
    require exact contracts, bounded resources, public mapping, fencing and conformance
    before runtime adoption.
evidence E2:
  |
    Foundation v3 F5 through F15 and character v5 R6 through R19 require
    adapter-owned focused proposals, transactional staging, complete server
    reconciliation, exceptional five-condition Q&A, source-based retry, cumulative
    resource limits, bounded stall and cycle detection, and trusted-state failure.
evidence E3:
  |
    Current code supplies typed V3 character and migration records, a six-request
    whole-candidate migration loop, family queues and fencing. These are reusable
    seams but do not implement the accepted focused-kernel topology.
evidence E4:
  |
    Corrected review of revision one found two substantive conflicts: its cluster-one-
    only skeleton ordering could not meet character R10 before fan-out, and its
    model-writable set_portrait operation conflicted with character R7 stable-ID
    authority. The owner selected REVISE for those two findings only.
evidence E5:
  |
    Accepted ADR0032 removes fixed whole-attempt wall clock as a semantic termination
    condition, separates provider transport, worker execution, semantic progress and
    cumulative resources, and leaves exact provider and worker time values pending
    route, platform, measurement, owner wait budget and exact policy acceptance.
evidence E6:
  |
    Exact review of revision three found one P1 defect: provider timeout did not close
    the timed-out request before replacement and did not define the run transition when
    zero recovery, exhausted recovery, failed admission, or lost fence prevents another
    provider call. The owner instructed REVISE.
evidence E7:
  |
    Exact review of revision four found two P1 defects: failed cumulative-resource
    admission was mislabeled as provider transport unavailability, and the current-
    fence path with no truthful recovery basis had no terminal transition. The owner
    instructed REVISE.
evidence E8:
  |
    Accepted foundation requirements allow failed attempts to end and explicit retry
    to reconstruct a new attempt from source without persisting intermediate model or
    worker checkpoints.
evidence E9:
  |
    On 2026-09-15 the product owner instructed relaxation after the proposed durable
    transport and worker Config identities were compared with stateless new-run retry.
evidence E10:
  |
    Formal review of exact design revision six and ADR0033 revision one reports PASS
    with no P0 through P3 findings. The review reasoning SHA256 is
    cdd328284fd76310ef28dfaff0f59c2e7cf559828c738a3a71c85839be1567d3.
evidence OWNER_ACCEPTANCE_REV6:
  |
    On 2026-09-15, immediately after the PASS result and complete Japanese review scope
    were presented, the product owner replied "ACCEPT". The reviewed design Markdown
    SHA256 was a0f2ba909d3c71fb7de2235bd84125b420663f6f8b5400c4236eaf92e832ebff,
    and this authoritative reasoning SHA256 before acceptance was
    69eb577b09356a3806fd08325a9c73b78e6c864aa78e2947c489dff706b69f6d.
premise C1:
  |
    Current public APIs, current-route limits, immutable generations and activation
    behavior must remain unchanged until later implementation and cutover decisions.
premise U1:
  |
    Provider quality, actual token economics, database DDL details, frontend layout,
    and runtime conformance are unverified. Exact provider timeout, worker lease and
    bounded transport-recovery settings remain undecided. The public compatibility
    mapping requires owner review.
decision D1 based_on P1, E1, E2, E3, C1, U1:
  |
    Use generic TypeScript kernel state with adapter-concrete source, candidate,
    obligation, work, proposal, finding, question, answer and final types. Decode
    unknown only at external boundaries and forbid JSON-string internal round trips
    and unchecked casts.
decision D2 based_on P1, E1, E2, C1:
  |
    Use server-registered Skill descriptors and work-item sessions exposing at most
    one bounded context query and one proposal or review Tool. Keep full schema and
    candidate server-side. Use adapter-owned closed proposal unions instead of
    generic JSON Patch. Bind common envelope cardinalities and text bounds, derive
    small writable character operations directly to CharacterDefinitionV3 member
    schemas, forbid a general cluster-five candidate write and model-writable portrait
    binding, and allow at most eight conflict-free operations inside the session
    cluster and computed semantic closure while the complete proposal stays within
    six KiB.
decision D3 based_on P1, E1, E2, E5, E8, E9, U1:
  |
    Propose semantic_authoring_policy_v1 with one concurrent request, eight LLM
    calls, forty-eight counted steps, 6000 input and 1500 output tokens per call,
    32000 cumulative input, 8000 cumulative output, 500000 micro-USD estimated cost,
    eight progress observations and two strategy changes. Bind pricing and
    token-estimator identities. Use separate route-specific provider transport Config
    and platform-specific worker lease Config, with positive millisecond values and zero
    or one transport recovery selected only by a later exact accepted policy. Keep each
    selected Config immutable for one live execution, but do not persist its generation
    or snapshot as run identity. Do not create a whole-attempt elapsed budget. Preserve
    the existing six-call route until a later cutover.
decision D4 based_on P1, E1, E2, U1:
  |
    Trigger information-gaining recovery after four no-progress steps and fail after
    three further no-progress steps. Detect three equal states in six observations
    and A-B-A-B in four. Allow adapter-declared temporary regressions for three
    observations without resetting resource or unresolved-cluster history.
decision D5 based_on P1, E1, E2, C1, U1:
  |
    Keep public attempt status values unchanged. Project needs-owner-answer as legacy
    failed plus AUTHORING_OWNER_ANSWER_REQUIRED and an additive ownerInteraction.
    Always append the answer and create a new source-reconstructed attempt rather
    than preserving a suspended checkpoint. Keep cancelled and expired as distinct
    control terminal states, and bind command replay to an exact request digest.
decision D6 based_on P1, E1, E2, E3, E5, E6, E7, E8, E9, C1:
  |
    Persist common run, request/accounting, question/answer and final-candidate
    metadata while family ports store typed final payloads. Every mutation compares
    owner, fencing token and run version. Provider timeout atomically terminalizes the
    old request under the current fence, retains its receipt and maximum reservation,
    and rejects its late result for candidate mutation. An admitted same-run recovery
    uses a new request identity and records its changed condition or materially
    different authorized request, derived by the server rather than asserted by the
    model. Under a current fence, zero recovery, consumed recovery, or absence of a
    truthful recovery basis fails the run with a recoverable provider-transport
    technical outcome. Only an otherwise-eligible recovery proceeds to resource
    admission; failed admission preserves the matching resource_exhausted outcome and
    exact exhausted dimensions. Both failure paths retain the timeout receipt,
    monotonic accounting and an owner new-run recipe. A handler that lost the fence
    writes nothing; the recovery owner applies process-or-lease-loss handling. Neither
    condition asserts semantic failure or resets accounting. Do not persist provider
    transport or worker execution Config generations or snapshots in the common run.
    A live same-run transport recovery uses its already selected immutable Config;
    process or lease loss ends the current run and a later explicit retry creates a new
    source-reconstructed run under then-current Config.
decision D7 based_on P1, E1, E2, C1:
  |
    Require black-box kernel conformance for character, battlefield and narration
    adapters, but wire only the character adapter in a later separately authorized
    runtime slice. Insert this reviewed design gate before the existing B6 task.
decision D8 based_on P1, E1, E2, E4, C1:
  |
    Define the character semantic-skeleton phase across cluster one and only the
    server-designated essential ability or limit and key-relationship claim slices
    from clusters two and three. Permit their focused operations before the checkpoint,
    then fan out remaining dependent work only after passage. Keep portrait binding
    outside the model operation union and preserve it from server authority unless a
    separately authorized server operation changes it.
decision ACCEPTANCE_REV6 based_on P1, E1, E2, E5, E8, E9, E10, OWNER_ACCEPTANCE_REV6, D1, D2, D3, D4, D5, D6, D7, D8:
  |
    Accept exact implementation-design revision six as the current design snapshot,
    superseding revision five only as the current design-acceptance target. Preserve
    undecided exact Config values and the implementation-stage obligation to retain a
    durable claim or expiry fact sufficient for lease-expiry recovery. This acceptance
    does not authorize implementation, commit, push, provider calls, deployment,
    migration, activation, rollback, or release.
